INSERT INTO `lessons` (`lesson_id`, `module_id`, `title`, `content`, `lesson_order`, `created_at`) VALUES
(1, 1, 'Definition and Scope', '##What is Urban Agriculture? \r\nUrban agriculture is the practice of cultivating crops, growing food,\r\nand raising livestock in urban and peri-urban settings. It includes producing, processing, and\r\ndistributing agricultural goods within city spaces to address food security, promote sustainable\r\nlivelihoods, and integrate agriculture into the urban economy.\r\n\r\n##Key Features:\r\n- **Utilizes Urban Spaces:** Urban agriculture makes innovative use of spaces like vacant \r\nlots, rooftops, balconies, walls, and even abandoned buildings to grow food and raise animals.\r\n- **Comprehensive Activities:** Beyond farming, it encompasses food processing (turning raw\r\ncrops into consumable products), distribution (transporting produce to consumers), and\r\nmarketing.\r\n- **Scales of Operation**: Practices range from small-scale home gardens to large commercial\r\nurban farms.\r\n\r\n##Categories:\r\n**1. Food Production:** Includes cultivating vegetables, fruits, herbs, aquaponics (combining fish farming\r\nand hydroponics), and hydroponics (growing plants without soil).\r\n\r\n**2. Livestock Production:** Small-scale raising of poultry, rabbits, or goats, and even urban beekeeping.\r\n\r\n**3. Ornamentals:** Growing flowers and landscaping plants for beautifying spaces, increasing\r\nbiodiversity, and promoting mental well-being.', 1, '2025-09-16 07:00:37'),
(2, 1, 'Historical Context', 'Urban agriculture has a long-standing history as a solution to food scarcity, particularly during\r\ntimes of crisis. Understanding its evolution helps recognize its importance in contemporary\r\nurban development.\r\n##Wartime Response (Victory Gardens)\r\nDuring World War II, governments promoted \"**Victory Gardens**\" in cities and\r\nsuburbs to supplement food rations. Citizens grew vegetables and fruits in\r\nbackyards, parks, and rooftops to ensure food security during supply chain\r\ndisruptions.\r\n##Traditional Systems in Asia\r\nUrban farming is deeply rooted in cultures such as India, where rooftop and\r\ncourtyard farming have been practiced for centuries. These systems not only\r\nsupplemented family diets but also reflected sustainable resource use, like\r\nrainwater harvesting.\r\n##Modern Evolution\r\nAs cities grew, urban agriculture adapted with innovations like vertical farming,\r\naquaponics, and greenhouses to maximize limited space while addressing urban\r\nchallenges like food deserts.', 2, '2025-09-16 09:58:12'),
(3, 1, 'Benefits of Urban Agriculture', 'Urban agriculture offers multifaceted benefits that address environmental, social, and economic\r\nchallenges faced by cities today.\r\n\r\n##Environmental Benefits\r\n- **Carbon Sequestration:** Urban farms absorb carbon dioxide, helping mitigate climate\r\nchange. Plants act as natural air purifiers by improving air quality in congested areas.\r\n\r\n- **Reduction of Urban Heat Island Effect:** Rooftop gardens and urban farms reduce heat\r\nbuildup in cities by insulating buildings and lowering surrounding temperatures,\r\ndecreasing energy demands for cooling.\r\n\r\n- **Biodiversity:** Urban gardens create habitats for pollinators like bees and butterflies,\r\nfostering biodiversity in cities.\r\n\r\n##Social Benefits\r\n- **Community Engagement:** Urban farming projects encourage community participation,\r\nempowering residents to work collaboratively. Community gardens foster a sense of\r\nownership and pride.\r\n\r\n- **Improved Well-Being:** Green spaces have proven psychological benefits, reduced stress\r\nand promoting relaxation in densely populated urban areas.\r\n\r\n- **Education and Awareness:** Urban farms serve as educational tools for teaching\r\nsustainable farming practices and healthy eating habits.\r\n\r\n##Economic Benefits\r\n- **Cost Savings:** Growing food locally reduces reliance on expensive imported produce,\r\nsaving households money.\r\n- **Job Creation:** Urban agriculture generates employment opportunities across farming, food\r\nprocessing, marketing, and distribution.\r\n- **Local Entrepreneurship:** Initiatives like farmers\' markets and agri-business startups\r\nencourage entrepreneurship and stimulate local economies. <br><br><br><br><br>\r\n\r\n**References:**\r\n- _Altieri, M. A., et al. (1999). The greening of the “barrios”: Urban agriculture for food security in Cuba. Agriculture and Human Values, 16(2), 131–140._\r\n- _Orsini, F., Kahane, R., Nono-Womdim, R., & Gianquinto, G. (2013). Urban agriculture:Another way to feed cities. Agriculture, Ecosystems & Environment, 198, 1–9._', 3, '2025-09-16 10:22:23'),
(5, 2, 'System Designs', 'Urban agriculture systems can be adapted to different spaces and needs. Below are various\r\nsystem designs, including landscaping and backyard gardening, that can be implemented in\r\nurban environments.\r\n\r\n## 1. Vertical Gardens\r\nVertical gardens are ideal for maximizing space in urban areas like balconies, walls, and small\r\nbackyards.\r\n\r\n### Examples of Vertical Gardens\r\n- **Wall-mounted planters:** Pots or containers attached to a vertical surface such as a\r\nfence, wall, or building side.\r\n\r\n- **Modular systems:** Pre-designed, stackable units that allow for efficient use of\r\nvertical space, often with slots for multiple plants.\r\n- **Living walls (green walls):** Vertical panels with integrated irrigation systems,\r\nsupporting climbing plants or small crops.\r\n- **Benefits**\r\n     - **Space-efficient:** Makes use of unused vertical space.\r\n     - **Aesthetic appeal:** Adds greenery to urban environments, improving the visual\r\n     environment.\r\n     - **Accessibility:** Easy maintenance and harvesting from elevated structures.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/hV6zGMhKMxM\" title=\"How to Create a Vertical Garden\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n## 2. Rooftop Gardens\r\nRooftops present an untapped resource for growing food in urban areas. Rooftop gardens can\r\nprovide a space for growing vegetables, fruits, and herbs while also offering environmental\r\nbenefits.\r\n\r\n**Key Design Features**\r\n     - **Lightweight soil media:** To avoid overloading the roof structure, use materials\r\n     like cocopeat, perlite, and vermiculite that are lighter than traditional soil.\r\n     - **Drainage systems:** Proper drainage is crucial to prevent waterlogging and leaks.\r\n     Using drainage mats, sloped beds, or built-in drainage systems ensures water\r\n     flows away from the roof.\r\n     - **Raised beds and containers:** Use raised beds or modular containers to make\r\n     gardening more manageable, provide structure, and optimize space.\r\n\r\n- **Advantages**\r\n     - **Urban heat island mitigation:** Rooftop gardens cool buildings and reduce the\r\n     surrounding heat, decreasing the need for air conditioning.\r\n     - **Insulation:** Green roofs provide insulation, reducing energy consumption in the\r\n     building below.\r\n\r\n## 3. Hydroponics and Aquaponics\r\nThese soilless techniques can be especially beneficial in urban environments where soil space is\r\nlimited or of poor quality.\r\n\r\n### Hydroponics\r\n- **Soilless cultivation:** In hydroponics, plants grow in a nutrient-rich water solution.  \r\n- **Example:** A small-scale system using PVC pipes arranged in tiers, where water\r\n  flows through the pipes, nourishing the plants.  \r\n- **Benefits:** Hydroponics requires less water than traditional farming and can be\r\n  easily scaled for small urban spaces.  \r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/rp7MeQYmm5k\" title=\"Got P500 to Invest? Learn Hydroponics Farming (TUTORIAL) | OG\"  frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n### Aquaponics\r\n- **Integrated system:** Combines hydroponics and fish farming, where fish waste\r\n  provides nutrients for plants, and plants help filter and purify the water for the\r\n  fish.  \r\n- **Example:** A small backyard system with a fish tank, plant grow beds, and a water\r\n  circulation system.  \r\n- **Benefits:** Sustainable food production that conserves water and reduces waste.', 2, '2025-09-16 12:14:26'),
(6, 2, 'System Designs (Part 2)', '##4. Landscaping for Urban Agriculture:\r\nLandscaping can be a functional and aesthetic addition to urban agriculture, enhancing both the\r\nbeauty and sustainability of the space.\r\n- **Examples of Landscaping Elements**\r\n     - **Edible landscapes:** Using ornamental plants that are also edible, such as **herbs**\r\n     (e.g., mint, basil), **edible flowers** (e.g., nasturtiums), or **fruit trees** (e.g., dwarf\r\n     apple or citrus).\r\n     - **Pollinator gardens:** Creating spaces to attract beneficial insects like **bees** and\r\n     **butterflies**. These gardens support biodiversity and ensure the pollination of\r\n     nearby food crops.\r\n     - **Companion planting in landscapes:** Strategic planting of crops that benefit each\r\n     other, such as planting basil near tomatoes to repel pests and enhance growth.\r\n- **Benefits:**\r\n     - **Aesthetic enhancement:** Green landscapes can beautify urban spaces, creating\r\n     tranquil environments in otherwise barren areas.\r\n     - **Sustainability:** Landscaping elements, such as **rain gardens**, can help manage\r\n     stormwater runoff and improve soil health.\r\n     - **Biodiversity promotion:** Pollinator and native plant gardens foster a healthy\r\n     ecosystem in urban areas, supporting wildlife and pest control.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/3frkqNjScpk\" title=\"Rain Gardens Explained in 2 minutes\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n##5. Backyard Gardening:\r\nBackyard gardens are a simple yet highly effective way to incorporate urban agriculture into\r\nresidential settings.\r\n\r\n- **Examples of Backyard Gardening:**\r\n    - **Traditional raised beds:** Use raised beds to grow vegetables like tomatoes, lettuce,\r\n     and carrots, which can be easily tended to and help prevent soil compaction.\r\n    - **Container gardens:** Use pots or containers to grow plants, especially in spaces\r\n     with limited ground area, such as patios or balconies. Containers can support\r\n     vegetables, herbs, and small fruit plants.\r\n    - **Composting systems:** Integrating composting into a backyard garden helps\r\n    recycle organic waste into nutrient-rich soil, enhancing plant health and\r\n    sustainability.\r\n- **Benefits**\r\n     - **Self-sufficiency:** Growing food in your backyard reduces reliance on external\r\n     food sources and promotes food security.\r\n     - **Improved diet:** Backyard gardens provide access to fresh, organic produce.\r\n     - **Engagement and wellness:** Gardening promotes physical activity, mental wellbeing, \r\n     and a deeper connection to nature.', 3, '2025-09-16 14:00:59'),
(7, 2, 'Resource Management in Design', 'Efficient resource use is vital to the sustainability of urban agriculture systems. \r\nRecycling and optimization are key strategies.\r\n\r\n##1. Recycling Materials\r\n- Urban agriculture can creatively use discarded materials to save costs and reduce waste.\r\n- **Examples:**\r\n     - Plastic bottles cut in half for seedling pots or vertical gardens.\r\n     - Old tires repurposed as planters for small crops.\r\n     - Wooden pallets converted into raised garden beds or vertical frames.\r\n\r\n##2. Water Optimization\r\nEfficient water management is critical in urban settings where water availability may be limited.\r\n     - **Rainwater Harvesting Systems**\r\n          - Install simple rainwater collection setups, such as barrels or tanks, connected to\r\n          rooftop gutters. Use collected rainwater for irrigation to reduce reliance on\r\n          municipal water supplies.\r\n     - **Drip Irrigation Systems**\r\n          - Drip irrigation delivers water directly to the plant roots through a network of\r\n          tubes, minimizing evaporation and runoff.\r\n          - **Example:** A rooftop garden equipped with a timer-controlled drip system for\r\n          consistent watering.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe  src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/1QYzMD78UJc\" title=\"Cheap, Quick and Easy Drip Irrigation System for Your Garden\"  frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n## 3.Nutrient Recycling\r\n- Use organic waste like vegetable peels and garden clippings to create compost, which can\r\nbe integrated into soil or used in vermicomposting systems.\r\n- **Nutrient-rich** water from aquaponics or collected rainwater can also be reused for\r\nirrigation.<br><br><br><br><br>\r\n\r\n**References:**\r\n- _Despommier, D. (2011). The Vertical Farm: Feeding the World in the 21st Century._\r\n- _Hynes, H. P. (1996). A Patch of Eden: America’s Inner-City Gardeners. Chelsea Green Publishing._', 4, '2025-09-16 14:10:22'),
(8, 3, 'Soil-Based Farming', 'Soil-based farming remains one of the most common practices in urban agriculture. Proper soil\r\nmanagement is key to optimizing plant growth and ensuring sustainable food production.\r\n\r\n## 1. Steps in Soil Preparation\r\n\r\n- **Soil Testing**\r\nBefore planting, soil testing is essential to understand the soil’s nutrient content and pH\r\nlevel.\r\n    - **pH Testing:** Soil should have a neutral pH **(6.0 to 7.0)** for most crops. If the soil is\r\n    too acidic (below 6.0), lime can be added to raise the pH. If it is too alkaline\r\n    (above 7.0), organic matter like compost can help lower the pH.\r\n    - **Nutrient Testing:** Tests for **macronutrients** (nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium) and\r\n    micronutrients (iron, calcium, magnesium) are important to determine if\r\n    additional fertilizers or amendments are needed.\r\n- **Adding Organic Matter**\r\nOrganic matter improves soil structure, increases water retention, and provides essential\r\nnutrients. Common types include:\r\n    - **Compost:** Decomposed organic material rich in nutrients.\r\n    - **Manure:** Animal waste, often from poultry, cows, or horses, that must be\r\n    composted before use to avoid introducing pathogens.\r\n    - **Cover Crops:** Planting legumes like clover or beans as green manure helps fix\r\n    nitrogen in the soil, improving fertility\r\n\r\n## 2. Crop Management\r\n\r\n- **Plant Spacing**\r\n\r\nProper spacing between plants is critical for optimizing resource use (sunlight, water, and\r\nnutrients). Crowded plants compete for these resources, leading to poor growth and\r\nincreased susceptibility to pests and diseases. Use guidelines for recommended spacing\r\nbased on the type of crops being grown.\r\n\r\n- **Mulching**\r\n\r\nMulching helps to conserve soil moisture, reduce weed growth, and improve soil quality.\r\nMulch can be organic (straw, grass clippings) or inorganic (plastic sheets). It also helps\r\nregulate soil temperature by protecting the roots from extreme heat or cold.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/UlGdc1o3510\" title=\"Types of MULCHING in Agriculture | Advantages of Mulching In Farming\"  frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>', 1, '2025-09-17 02:55:34'),
(9, 3, 'Soilless Farming Techniques', 'Soilless farming is an innovative method for growing plants without traditional soil, using water\r\nand nutrient solutions. These techniques are particularly useful in urban settings where soil may\r\nbe limited, contaminated, or unavailable.\r\n\r\n## 1. Hydroponics\r\nHydroponics is the practice of growing plants in a water-based, nutrient-rich solution without\r\nsoil.\r\n- **Steps to Build a Deep-Water Culture System**\r\n    - **Container:** Use a container to hold water (e.g., a large plastic bin).\r\n    - **Air Pump and Air Stone:** Place an air pump at the bottom to oxygenate the water.\r\n    An air stone connected to the pump helps to distribute oxygen evenly throughout\r\n    the solution.\r\n    - **Net Pots and Grow Media:** Use net pots to hold the plants. Place a growing\r\n    medium (such as expanded clay pellets or perlite) inside the net pots to support\r\n    the plant roots.\r\n   - **Nutrient Solution:** Mix hydroponic nutrients with water. This solution provides\r\n    essential nutrients like nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium, calcium, and magnesium.\r\n    - **Water Circulation:** Ensure water circulates through the system to allow plants to\r\n    absorb nutrients efficiently. In a Deep-Water Culture (DWC) system, plants\' roots\r\n    are submerged in the nutrient solution, which they can directly absorb.\r\n\r\n- **Nutrient Requirements for Hydroponic Vegetables**\r\n\r\nHydroponic plants require a balanced nutrient solution containing:\r\n      ![](../images/lessons/Macronutrients.png)\r\n    - Regular monitoring and adjusting of the pH (usually **between 5.5 and 6.5**) and\r\n    nutrient levels is necessary to ensure optimal plant growth.\r\n\r\n## 2. Aeroponics\r\nAeroponics is a soilless growing technique where plant roots are suspended in air and misted\r\nwith a nutrient solution.\r\n\r\n- **Mist System**\r\n\r\n    A mist or fine spray is used to deliver nutrients and water directly to the plant roots. The\r\n    mist allows roots to absorb moisture and nutrients without being submerged in liquid.\r\n    - **Setup:** Typically involves a chamber or enclosed structure with hanging plants\r\n    and a misting system that delivers nutrient-laden water to the roots.\r\n- **Benefits of Aeroponics**\r\n    - **Faster Growth Rates:** Due to the high oxygen availability around the roots, plants\r\n    in aeroponic systems often grow faster than those in soil or hydroponic systems.\r\n    - **Water Efficiency:** Aeroponics uses less water than traditional farming methods\r\n    and hydroponics, as the mist system uses minimal amounts of water, and excess\r\n    water is often recycled.\r\n    - **Space Efficiency:** Aeroponics systems can be stacked vertically, making them\r\n    ideal for small urban spaces.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/IInjYsqRXmg\" title=\"Which Method Reigns Supreme: Hydroponics or Aeroponics\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>', 2, '2025-09-17 03:02:21'),
(10, 3, 'Composting and Resource Recycling ( Part 1 )', 'Composting and resource recycling are critical components of urban agriculture, allowing\r\ncommunities to recycle organic waste into nutrient-rich soil amendments. This process reduces\r\nwaste sent to landfills, improves soil health, and supports sustainable food production.\r\n\r\n## Types of Composting\r\n\r\n- **1. Aerobic Composting (Requires Oxygen)** \r\n\r\nAerobic composting occurs when organic material decomposes in the presence \r\nof oxygen. It relies on microorganisms, such as bacteria and fungi, that require \r\noxygen to break down organic matter.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/8VrS12R1_vY\" title=\"Aerobic Compost - How does it work?\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n- **Traditional Aerobic Composting**\r\n\r\n    - **Process:** Involves layering organic waste (green materials like food scraps, grass\r\n    clippings, and brown materials like leaves, straw, or cardboard) in a compost pile or bin.\r\n    The pile must be turned regularly (every few weeks) to ensure oxygen reaches all parts\r\n    and to accelerate decomposition.\r\n    - **Benefits:** Produces high-quality compost with a balance of nutrients that enriches soil.\r\n    - **Timeframe:** Typically takes 3-6 months to fully decompose, depending on pile size and\r\n    environmental factors (e.g., temperature and moisture).\r\n- **Rapid Aerobic Composting**\r\n    - **Process:** This method speeds up the composting process by maintaining a high\r\n    temperature (**50-70°C** or **122-158°F**) and regular turning of the compost pile. Adding\r\n    nitrogen-rich materials (e.g., fresh grass, manure) and ensuring proper moisture helps\r\n    create the ideal conditions for heat-loving microorganisms.\r\n    - **Benefits:** Accelerates the breakdown of organic material in a shorter time frame (2-3\r\n    weeks).\r\n    - **Considerations:** Requires frequent monitoring of temperature and moisture levels to\r\n    maintain optimal conditions.\r\n- **Hot Composting (Temperature-Controlled Method)**\r\n    - **Process:** The compost pile is kept at higher temperatures (above **55°C** or **131°F**) for a\r\n    period of time, which kills pathogens, weed seeds, and accelerates decomposition.\r\n    - **Benefits:** Results in quick decomposition and cleaner compost. Ideal for large amounts of\r\n    waste.\r\n    - **Considerations:** Needs careful management of moisture, aeration, and carbon-to-nitrogen\r\n    ratio. It can require more frequent turning.\r\n- **Bin or Tumbler Composting**\r\n    - **Process:** Using a compost bin or tumbler to contain and rotate compost. The enclosed\r\n    space retains heat and moisture, making it easier to manage the process.\r\n    - **Benefits:** More compact and neat compared to open piles. Easier to turn with a tumbler.\r\n    - **Considerations:** Can be smaller, making it less suitable for large quantities of waste.', 3, '2025-09-17 03:14:05'),
(11, 3, 'Composting and Resource Recycling ( Part 2 )', '## Types of Composting\r\n\r\n- **2. Anaerobic Composting (Without Oxygen)**\r\n\r\nAnaerobic composting takes place in low-oxygen environments. Microorganisms\r\nthat thrive without oxygen (**anaerobes**) break down the organic matter in these systems.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/9m3jNmsB5Lw\" title=\"A Beginner&#39;s Guide to Bokashi Composting\"  frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n- **Bokashi Composting**\r\n\r\n    - **Process:** A Japanese method where food scraps (including cooked food and dairy) are\r\n    fermented using a special inoculant made of beneficial microorganisms. The scraps are\r\n    placed in an airtight container, and the Bokashi mix is added to encourage fermentation.\r\n    - **Benefits:** Can compost a wider variety of organic materials (e.g., meat, dairy) compared\r\n    to other composting methods. Produces a fermented product that can be added to soil or\r\n    used as a soil amendment.\r\n    - **Considerations:** Requires a sealed container and patience for fermentation. The final\r\n    product is not fully composted and must be buried or added to a compost pile for further\r\n    decomposition.\r\n\r\n- **Pit Composting**\r\n\r\n    - **Process:** Organic waste is buried in a pit or trench where anaerobic decomposition occurs.\r\n    It is often used for large-scale organic waste management.\r\n    - **Benefits:** Simple and low-maintenance; suitable for disposing of large amounts of organic\r\n    waste.\r\n    - **Considerations:** Longer decomposition times and possible odors if not properly managed.\r\n\r\n- **Tumbler or Closed-Container Anaerobic Composting**\r\n\r\n    - **Process:** A sealed container or tumbler is used to contain organic waste, where anaerobic\r\n    conditions are maintained. Some systems incorporate the use of microorganisms or\r\n    inoculants to enhance fermentation.\r\n    - **Benefits:** More contained and odor-controlled compared to pit composting.\r\n    - **Considerations** Requires a closed system, making it more manageable but slower than\r\n    aerobic methods.', 4, '2025-09-17 03:19:41'),
(12, 3, 'Composting and Resource Recycling ( Part3 )', '## Types of Composting\r\n\r\n**3. Vermicomposting (Using Earthworms)**\r\n\r\nVermicomposting uses worms (often red wiggler worms) to break down organic waste into high quality compost. \r\nThe worms consume organic material, producing castings (worm manure) that are rich in plant nutrients.\r\n\r\n- **Process:** Create a worm bin using bedding (e.g., shredded newspaper, coconut coir) and\r\nadd food scraps like fruit and vegetable peels. The worms digest the organic waste and\r\nproduce nutrient-rich castings.\r\n- **Benefits:** The resulting vermicompost is high in essential nutrients like nitrogen,\r\nphosphorus, and potassium, which are excellent for soil health.\r\n- **Considerations:** Vermicomposting requires a controlled environment (cool, moist, and\r\ndark) and may not be suitable for large-scale composting.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/6Ejq4pbANww\" title=\"How to Make Vermicompost | How To make Vermicompost at Home From Kitchen Waste\"  frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n**4. Black Soldier Fly (BSF) Composting**\r\n\r\nBlack soldier flies are larvae of the black soldier fly (**Hermetia illucens**) and are known for their\r\nability to decompose organic waste rapidly. They are commonly used in waste-to-nutrient\r\nsystems.\r\n\r\n- **Process:** Organic waste (e.g., food scraps, manure) is placed in a container where BSF\r\nlarvae consume the waste and convert it into high-protein biomass. The larvae can later\r\nbe harvested and used as feed for animals, while the remaining composted material can\r\nbe used to enrich soil.\r\n- **Benefits:**\r\n    - Rapid decomposition of organic waste.\r\n    - Larvae are a high-protein animal feed, making the system a dual-purpose solution.\r\n    - Efficient conversion of waste into usable products.\r\n- **Considerations:** Requires a warm environment for optimal larvae growth (**25-35°C** or **77-\r\n95°F**). Careful management of the system is needed to prevent odors and ensure proper\r\nwaste flow.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe  src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/qc7xCVuJJCs\" title=\"Black Soldier Fly Farming at Home: How to Set Up a BSF Composting Bin\"  frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n**5. Recycling Waste for Fertilizer Production**\r\n\r\nUsing kitchen scraps and other organic waste materials for composting and fertilizer production\r\nhelps reduce the volume of waste in landfills while creating valuable resources for urban\r\nagriculture. Simple methods like composting food scraps, yard waste, and organic materials such\r\nas coffee grounds, eggshells, and fruit peels can result in nutrient-dense compost, which can be\r\ndirectly added to soil or used as part of a vermicomposting or BSF system.<br><br><br><br><br>\r\n\r\n**References:**\r\n- _FAO. (2017). Urban and peri-urban agriculture: Guidelines for policymakers._\r\n- _Hynes, H. P. (1996). A Patch of Eden: America’s Inner-City Gardeners. Chelsea Green Publishing._\r\n- _Orsini, F., Kahane, R., Nono-Womdim, R., & Gianquinto, G. (2013). Urban agriculture:Another way to feed cities. Agriculture, Ecosystems & Environment, 198, 1-9._', 5, '2025-09-17 03:25:48'),
(13, 4, 'Common Pests and Diseases in Urban Farming ( Part 1 )', '## Pests \r\n\r\n**Pests** are organisms that damage crops by feeding on them, transmitting diseases, or\r\ndisturbing their growth processes. Here are some common pests found in urban farming,\r\nespecially in vegetables:\r\n\r\n- **Aphids:** Small, soft-bodied insects that suck sap from plants, leading to stunted growth,\r\ncurled leaves, and reduced plant vitality. Common on leafy greens like lettuce and\r\nspinach.\r\n- **Spider Mites:** Tiny arachnids that cause speckled, discolored, and damaged leaves,\r\nparticularly on crops like tomatoes, peppers, and beans. They thrive in hot, dry\r\nconditions.\r\n- **Whiteflies:** Small, flying insects that feed on plant sap, often found on the undersides of\r\nleaves. They can transmit viral diseases and lead to yellowing and wilting.\r\n- **Slugs and Snails:** These pests feed on leaves, stems, and fruit, creating irregular holes and\r\nsilvery trails on plants like lettuce, cabbage, and other leafy vegetables.\r\n- **Cabbage Worms (Imported Cabbageworm):** The larvae of a white butterfly, these worms\r\nchew on the leaves of cabbage, cauliflower, and other brassicas, damaging the plant.\r\n- **Thrips:** Tiny, winged insects that cause streaking or silvering on leaves and flowers,\r\nparticularly affecting tomatoes, peppers, and strawberries.\r\n- **Root-Knot Nematodes:** Microscopic worms that infect the roots of plants like tomatoes\r\nand peppers, causing swelling or galls, leading to reduced water and nutrient uptake.\r\n                        <a href=\'https://www.fix.com/blog/common-garden-pests-and-how-to-get-rid-of-them/?srsltid=AfmBOor4dh_yVXdg6HR0eOlM3iG0Uz8-LS4_A8M5DEBnJQnZM9NsEZRY\'><img src=\"https://fixcom-g4bhetdmcgd9b7er.z01.azurefd.net/assets/content/15641/which-insects-benefit-your-garden-001.png\" alt=\"Insects That Benefit The Garden\" border=0 /></a>\r\n                        <br />\r\n                        Source: <a href=\"https://www.fix.com/blog/\">Fix.com Blog</a>', 1, '2025-09-17 03:32:53'),
(14, 4, 'Common Pests and Diseases in Urban Farming ( Part 2 )', '## Diseases\r\n\r\n**Plant diseases** are caused by various pathogens like fungi, bacteria, viruses, and\r\nnematodes. Common vegetable diseases in urban agriculture include:\r\n\r\n- **Powdery Mildew:** A fungal disease that appears as white, powdery spots on leaves. It\r\naffects many crops like cucumbers, squash, and tomatoes, reducing photosynthesis and\r\nweakening plants.\r\n- **Bacterial Blight:** Caused by bacteria, this disease results in water-soaked lesions on\r\nleaves, stems, and fruits. It is common in tomatoes, peppers, and beans and can spread\r\nrapidly in humid conditions.\r\n- **Downy Mildew:** A fungal-like disease that affects plants like lettuce, spinach, and\r\ncucumbers. It causes yellowing and distortion of leaves, especially under high humidity.\r\n- **Fusarium Wilt:** A soil-borne fungal disease that causes wilting, yellowing of leaves, and\r\ndeath of the plant. Common in tomatoes, eggplants, and peppers.\r\n- **Tomato Blight (Early and Late Blight):** Fungal infections that cause dark, water-soaked\r\nlesions on leaves, stems, and fruits. Early blight affects older foliage, while late blight\r\nspreads rapidly under wet, cool conditions.\r\n- **Root Rot (Phytophthora or Pythium):** Caused by water mold, root rot affects plants in\r\npoorly drained soils and leads to yellowing, wilting, and eventual death of the plant.\r\n\r\n     <a><img src=\"https://fixcom-g4bhetdmcgd9b7er.z01.azurefd.net/assets/content/30476/detecting-garden-diseases-embed-large.png\" alt=\"How to Detect and Prevent Garden Diseases\" border=0 /></a><br /> Source: <a href=\"https://www.fix.com/blog/\">Fix.com Blog</a>', 2, '2025-09-17 03:34:25'),
(15, 4, 'Management Practices for Pest and Disease Control ( Part 1 )', '## Biological Control\r\n\r\nBiological control involves the use of natural enemies of pests to regulate\r\ntheir populations. This is an eco-friendly way of pest management that avoids chemical\r\npesticides.\r\n\r\n- **Beneficial Insects**\r\n\r\n    - **Ladybugs (Ladybird Beetles):** Natural predators of aphids, mealybugs, and other softbodied pests.\r\n    - **Lacewing Larvae:** Lacewings are excellent predators of aphids, thrips, and mealybugs.\r\n    Their larvae can consume large numbers of pests, making them effective in pest control\r\n    for crops like lettuce, tomatoes, and other leafy vegetables.\r\n    - **Predatory Mites:** These mites feed on pest mites, helping to control spider mite\r\n    populations in crops like beans and cucumbers.\r\n    - **Parasitic Wasps:** Wasps like Trichogramma spp. parasitize the eggs of pest insects, such\r\n    as moths, and can help control caterpillar pests.\r\n\r\n- **Entomopathogenic Fungi and Bacteria**\r\n\r\n    - **Beauveria bassiana:** A naturally occurring fungus that targets a wide range of pests,\r\n    including aphids, whiteflies, and beetles. When applied to crops, it infects and kills the\r\n    pests without harming beneficial insects or plants.\r\n    - **Metarhizium anisopliae:** Another beneficial fungus that infects and kills insects such as\r\n    termites, beetles, and ants. It works similarly to Beauveria bassiana and can be applied to\r\n    crops to control pests like root weevils and other soil-borne insects.\r\n    - **Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt):** A bacterium that controls caterpillar pests by producing a\r\n    toxin that is toxic to insect larvae but harmless to humans, animals, and beneficial insects.\r\n\r\n- **Microbial Biological control agent**\r\n\r\n    - **Trichoderma spp:** This beneficial fungus is used in soil health management and to\r\n    control plant diseases like root rot caused by pathogens such as Pythium and Fusarium. It\r\n    also promotes plant growth by outcompeting harmful soil fungi, acting as a natural\r\n    biocontrol agent.', 3, '2025-09-17 03:39:40'),
(16, 4, 'Management Practices for Pest and Disease Control ( Part 2 )', '## Cultural Practices\r\n\r\nCultural practices involve managing the growing environment to reduce\r\npest and disease pressures.\r\n\r\n- **Crop Rotation:** Rotating crops each season reduces the likelihood of pests and diseases\r\nthat favor certain plant families. For instance, avoiding planting tomatoes in the same\r\nspot as last year can prevent soil-borne pathogens like Fusarium wilt.\r\n- **Intercropping:** Growing different plant species together helps confuse pests and reduces\r\npest attraction to any one crop. For example, intercropping basil with tomatoes can help\r\ndeter pests like aphids and whiteflies.\r\n- **Resistant Varieties:** Select vegetable varieties that are resistant to common pests and\r\ndiseases. For example, certain tomato varieties are resistant to late blight, while other\r\nvegetables may have natural resistance to aphids or mildew.\r\n- **Proper Spacing:** Overcrowding can create a microclimate that is conducive to disease.\r\nProper spacing ensures adequate airflow, reducing humidity levels and the risk of\r\ndiseases like powdery mildew.\r\n- **Sanitation:** Regularly remove and dispose of infected plants or leaves to prevent the\r\nspread of pests and pathogens. Clean tools and equipment to avoid cross-contamination.', 4, '2025-09-17 03:43:06'),
(17, 4, 'Management Practices for Pest and Disease Control ( Part 3 )', '## Organic Pesticides\r\n\r\nOrganic pesticides are a safer alternative to synthetic chemicals and are\r\ngenerally less toxic to humans, animals, and beneficial insects.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/tgf_SHvvrTQ\" title=\"BAKING SODA Powerful ORGANIC Pesticide Mix!\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n- **Neem Oil:** Derived from the neem tree, neem oil is an organic pesticide that repels and\r\nkills pests like aphids, whiteflies, and spider mites. It works as an insect growth regulator,\r\npreventing pest larvae from maturing.\r\n- **Garlic Spray:** Garlic is a natural insect repellent. A garlic solution (blended garlic and\r\nwater) can deter aphids, whiteflies, and beetles.\r\n- **Chili Pepper Extract:** A strong spray made from hot peppers can deter a wide range of\r\npests, including aphids, caterpillars, and ants. The capsaicin in the peppers irritates pests,\r\nkeeping them away from plants.\r\n- **Diatomaceous Earth:** Made from fossilized remains of algae, diatomaceous earth is a fine\r\npowder that can be sprinkled around plants to control soft-bodied pests like slugs, snails,\r\nand aphids. It works by desiccating the pests.\r\n- **Insecticidal Soap:** Made from potassium salts, insecticidal soap is effective against softbodied \r\npests like aphids, whiteflies, and mealybugs. It disrupts the cell membranes of pests and dries them out.', 5, '2025-09-17 03:44:44'),
(18, 4, 'Integrated Pest Management (IPM) for Vegetables', 'IPM combines multiple pest management strategies to minimize the impact of pests and diseases\r\non crops.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/_R0My-2ag94\" title=\"WHAT IS INTEGRATED PEST MANAGEMENT (IPM)?\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n- **Monitoring:** Regularly inspect crops for signs of pests and diseases. Use tools like sticky\r\ntraps, visual checks, and soil tests to detect early infestations.\r\n- **Thresholds:** Determine pest population levels where control is needed. If pest numbers\r\nare below the threshold, no action is necessary. If they exceed the threshold, control\r\nmeasures are implemented.\r\n- **Physical Barriers:** Use row covers or netting to prevent pests like aphids and cabbage\r\nworms from accessing plants. Netting can also protect crops from flying insects like\r\nwhiteflies.\r\n- **Trap Cropping:** Grow a pest-attracting plant (e.g., marigolds for aphids or mustard for\r\ncabbage worms) near your vegetables. Pests are drawn to the trap crop, protecting your\r\nmain crops.\r\n- **Companion Planting:** Certain plants repel pests or attract beneficial insects. For example,\r\nplanting basil with tomatoes can deter whiteflies, and marigolds can help repel\r\nnematodes.<br><br><br><br><br>\r\n\r\n**References:**\r\n- _Altieri, M. A., & Nicholls, C. I. (1995). Biodiversity and pest management in agroecosystems. CRC Press._', 6, '2025-09-17 03:45:44'),
(19, 5, 'Factors Influencing Crop Selection (Part 1)', '## Climate\n\nUrban microclimates are often distinct from rural areas, which can affect the growth and yield of\ncrops. In Baguio City, the temperate climate plays a significant role in determining which crops\nare best suited for cultivation\n\n- **Temperature:**\n![](../images/lessons/temperature.png)\n- **Rainfall:** \n![](../images/lessons/rainfall.png)\n- **Microclimates:** \n![](../images/lessons/microclimates.png)', 1, '2025-09-17 03:51:35'),
(20, 5, 'Factors Influencing Crop Selection ( Part 2 )', '## Space Constraints\n\nUrban areas like Baguio City often have limited space for farming, especially in densely\npopulated regions. Therefore, choosing crops that can yield high results in small spaces or\nvertical spaces is essential.\n\n- **Vertical Farming:** Growing climbing crops like beans, peas, and tomatoes on trellises or\nusing vertical hydroponics can help maximize limited space.\n- **High-Yield, Low-Space Crops:** **Leafy vegetables** (e.g., lettuce, kale), **herbs** (e.g., basil,\ncilantro), and **small fruits** (e.g., strawberries, dwarf varieties of tomatoes) can be grown\nefficiently in compact spaces, such as containers, window boxes, and small garden beds.\n\n## Market Demand and Nutritional Value\n\nWhen selecting crops for urban farming, it\'s essential to consider local market demand and\nnutritional value. This ensures the crops are not only suited to local conditions but also meet\nconsumer needs.\n\n- **Local Restaurants and Markets:** Baguio City has a thriving food culture, with restaurants\nseeking fresh, local produce. Crops like basil, lettuce, tomatoes, and culinary herbs are\nalways in demand.\n- **Nutritional Value:** Prioritize crops that provide nutritional benefits, such as **leafy greens**\n(rich in vitamins A, C, and K) and **root vegetables** (good sources of carbohydrates and\nfiber).', 2, '2025-09-17 03:54:00'),
(21, 5, 'Seasonal Crop Calendars for Baguio City ( Part 1)', 'A seasonal crop calendar helps farmers plan their crops to ensure year-round production,\r\nmaximize yields, and manage pest cycles effectively. The following steps outline the process for\r\ncreating a seasonal crop calendar tailored to the specific conditions of Baguio City.\r\n\r\n## Key Steps in Planning\r\n\r\n- **1. Identify Growing Seasons Based on Local Climatic Data**\r\n    - Baguio City experiences distinct dry and wet seasons. Knowing the timing and\r\n    duration of these seasons helps determine which crops are best suited for specific\r\n    months.\r\n    - Cool season crops are typically planted during the dry months (**November to\r\n    February**), while warm season crops are planted during the warmer, wetter\r\n    months (**March to June**).\r\n- **2. Plan for Crop Rotations**\r\n    - To maintain soil health and prevent pest build-up, practice crop rotation. For\r\n    instance, after growing nitrogen-demanding crops like beans, rotate with crops\r\n    like carrots or tomatoes that don’t deplete soil nutrients as quickly.\r\n- **3. Account for Crop Maturity Periods**\r\n    - To ensure staggered harvests and minimize waste, plan for crops with varying\r\n    maturity periods. For example, plant radishes (quick-growing) alongside broccoli\r\n    (longer growing period) to harvest different crops at different times.', 3, '2025-09-17 03:58:28'),
(22, 5, 'Seasonal Crop Calendars for Baguio City (Part 2)', '## Example Crop Calendar for Baguio City\r\n![](../images/lessons/BaguioCrops.png)', 4, '2025-09-17 04:01:53'),
(23, 5, 'Techniques for Maximizing Yields Across Different Seasons', '## 1. Greenhouses and Shade Netting\r\n\r\n- In Baguio, temperature fluctuations can sometimes affect crops. Using\r\n**greenhouses** or shade nets can help manage temperature, protect plants from\r\npests, and extend the growing season.\r\n\r\n##  2. Raised Beds and Containers\r\n\r\n- Utilize **raised beds** for better soil drainage, which is especially helpful during the\r\nrainy season. **Container gardening** is another space-saving technique that allows\r\nfarmers to grow crops like tomatoes, peppers, and herbs in compact urban spaces.\r\n\r\n## 3. Water Management\r\n\r\n-  Install **drip irrigation systems** to ensure efficient water delivery during dry periods\r\nand prevent waterlogging during the rainy season.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden;\">\r\n  <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/l_5Y7FdTzYg\" title=\"The Best Raised Garden Bed Option for you!\"\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen\r\n          style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"></iframe>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n<br><br><br><br><br>\r\n\r\n**References:**\r\n- _Lal, R. (2004). Soil carbon sequestration to mitigate climate change. Geoderma, 123(1),1–22_\r\n- _Pande, C., Joshi, A., & Tewari, A. (2020). Rainwater harvesting: An approach towardswater conservation. Water Management, 34(2), 78–85._', 5, '2025-09-17 04:03:42'),
(55, 21, 'Understanding Organic Fertilizer', 'Plants need nutrients to grow, develop healthy leaves and roots, and produce flowers, fruits, and seeds. These nutrients are naturally found in soil, but continuous planting can gradually reduce the nutrients available to plants. **Fertilizers** can help supply nutrients and maintain soil productivity.\n\n**Organic fertilizers** are fertilizers made from materials that come from plants, animals, or other naturally occurring organic sources. Examples include *compost*, *animal manure*, *vermicompost*, and *decomposed plant materials*.\n\nUnlike **synthetic fertilizers**, which are manufactured or processed to provide specific concentrations of nutrients, **organic fertilizers** are generally derived from natural materials. Their nutrient content can vary depending on the materials used and how they were processed.\n\n## Why Is Organic Fertilizer Important?\n\n**Organic fertilizer** can provide several benefits to an urban garden. When organic materials are properly decomposed and applied, they can contribute nutrients and organic matter to the soil.\n\nOrganic fertilizers can also help:\n    - Improve soil structure\n    - Increase the soil\'s ability to retain moisture\n    - Support beneficial soil organisms\n    - Recycle household and garden organic waste\n    - Reduce the amount of organic waste sent to landfills\n    - Support more sustainable gardening practices\n\nHowever, **organic fertilizer** is **not automatically better** simply because it is natural. It still needs to be properly prepared and applied. Improperly handled materials   can contain pathogens, weed seeds, excessive nutrients, or other contaminants.\n\n## Organic Fertilizer vs. Synthetic Fertilizer\n\n![](../images/lessons/lesson_6a76e6c283236.png)\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 1, '2026-08-08 06:17:39');
INSERT INTO `lessons` (`lesson_id`, `module_id`, `title`, `content`, `lesson_order`, `created_at`) VALUES
(58, 21, 'Types and Sources of Organic Fertilizer (Part 1)', '**Organic fertilizers** can come from different natural sources, including *plants*, *animals*, and *decomposed organic materials*. Each type has different characteristics and may be used in different ways depending on the needs of the plants and soil.\nUnderstanding the different types of organic fertilizer helps urban gardeners choose suitable materials for their plants and use them responsibly.\n\n## 1. Compost\n\n![](../images/lessons/lesson_6a76f3ac3176b.jpg)\n\n**Compost** is an organic material produced through the controlled decomposition of plant and other suitable organic materials.\n\nCommon materials used for composting include:\n     - Vegetable and fruit scraps\n     - Dried leaves\n     - Grass clippings\n     - Plant residues\n     - Shredded untreated paper or cardboard\n\nAs these materials decompose, *microorganisms* break them down into a dark, crumbly material that can be added to soil.\n\n**Benefits of Compost**:\n     - Add organic matter to soil\n     - Improve soil structure\n     - Help the soil retain moisture\n     - Support soil organisms\n     - Recycle suitable household and garden waste\n\nCompost is particularly useful for urban farming because it allows gardeners to turn some organic household waste into a useful resource.\n\n## 2. Vermicompost\n\n![](../images/lessons/lesson_6a76f6aa0a83c.jpg)\n\n**Vermicompost** is produced with the help of earthworms. In a suitable vermicomposting system, earthworms consume organic materials and produce worm castings, which can be used as an organic soil amendment. Vermicomposting can be suitable for urban households because it can be done in relatively small containers.\n\nBenefits of Vermicompost:\n     - Add organic matter to growing media\n     - Provide plant nutrients\n     - Support soil biological activity\n     - Make use of suitable food and plant waste\n\nHowever, vermicomposting requires *proper moisture*, *airflow*, *suitable materials*, and *appropriate conditions* for the earthworms.\n\n## 3. Animal Manure\n\n![](../images/lessons/lesson_6a76f7883c7bb.jpg)\n\n**Animal manure** is organic material produced by livestock and other farm animals. Examples include manure from:\n     - Chickens\n     - Cattle\n     - Goats\n     - Horses\n\nAnimal manure can contain nutrients that plants need. However, fresh manure should **not** simply be placed directly around edible crops.\nProper composting or treatment is important because improperly handled manure may contain *pathogens*, *weed seeds*, or *excessive nutrients*.\n\nSafe Use of Animal Manure\n     - Use properly treated or composted manure.\n     - Follow appropriate application practices.\n     - Avoid contaminating harvested crops.\n     - Store manure properly.\n     - Prevent runoff into waterways.\n\n**Proper handling** is especially important when growing vegetables and other crops that people will eat.\n\n[checkpoint:eyJ0eXBlIjoibXVsdGlfc2VsZWN0IiwicXVlc3Rpb24iOiJXaGljaCBvcmdhbmljIGZlcnRpbGl6ZXIgaXMgcHJvZHVjZWQgd2l0aCB0aGUgaGVscCBvZiBlYXJ0aHdvcm1zPyIsIm9wdGlvbnMiOnsiQSI6IkFuaW1hbCBtYW51cmUiLCJCIjoiVmVybWljb21wb3N0IiwiQyI6IlN5bnRoZXRpYyBmZXJ0aWxpemVyIiwiRCI6IkNoZW1pY2FsIHBlc3RpY2lkZSJ9LCJjb3JyZWN0IjpbIkIiXX0=]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 2, '2026-08-08 09:10:10'),
(59, 21, 'Types and Sources of Organic Fertilizer (Part 2)', '## 4. Plant-Based Organic Fertilizer\n\n![](../images/lessons/lesson_6a76fbacc5406.png)\n\n**Plant materials** can also become useful sources of organic nutrients and organic matter.\n\nExamples include:\n     - Dried leaves\n     - Grass clippings\n     - Crop residues\n     - Plant trimmings\n     - Other suitable plant materials\n\nThese materials can be composted or processed into other organic soil amendments. Instead of immediately throwing away suitable plant waste, urban gardeners can reuse some of it to support their gardens.\n\n**Example:**\nA household grows lettuce and regularly removes old leaves and other plant materials.\nInstead of throwing all of the plant waste into the trash, suitable materials can be added to a composting system. After decomposition, the resulting compost can be used to improve the soil for future crops.\n\nThis creates a simple cycle: \nPlant Waste → Compost → Soil → New Plants\n\n## 5. Organic Liquid Fertilizers\n\nSome organic materials can be processed into liquid fertilizers or nutrient solutions.\nThese may be made from **plant-based** or other** organic materials**. Their nutrient concentration depends on the ingredients and preparation method.\n\n**Organic liquid fertilizers** should be used carefully. A solution that is **too concentrated**may damage plants, while an improperly prepared product may not provide the expected nutrients.\n\nFor this reason, gardeners should follow reliable preparation and application instructions rather than assuming that any homemade organic mixture is safe.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 3, '2026-08-08 09:37:31'),
(60, 21, 'Choosing an Organic Fertilizer', 'Different plants and growing conditions may require different approaches. Before choosing an organic fertilizer, consider the following:\n\n## 1. Type of Plant\n\nDifferent crops have different nutrient requirements. A leafy vegetable may have different needs from a fruiting crop.\n\n## 2. Soil Condition\n\nThe condition of the soil can affect which type of organic amendment is appropriate.\n\n## 3. Nutrient Requirements\n\nGardeners should consider what nutrients the plants need instead of applying fertilizer simply because it is available.\n\n## 4. Quality of the Material\n\n[youtube:Organic fertilizer should be properly prepared, mature, and suitable for its intended use.]\n\nOrganic fertilizer should be properly prepared, mature, and suitable for its intended use.\n\n## 5. Amount to Apply\n\nUsing too much fertilizer does not necessarily produce healthier plants. Excess nutrients can be wasted and may contribute to environmental problems.\n\n## Choosing the Right Type\n\nThere is **no** single organic fertilizer that is best for every situation.\n\nFor example:\n**Compost** may be useful when the goal is to add organic matter and improve soil condition.\n\n**Vermicompost** may be suitable for gardeners who have access to earthworms and want to recycle suitable organic household waste.\n\n**Animal manure** can provide nutrients but requires proper treatment and handling.\n\n**Plant-based** materials can be composted and returned to the soil.\n\nThe most appropriate choice depends on the *crop*, *soil condition*, *available materials*, and *intended use*.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 4, '2026-08-08 09:53:22'),
(61, 21, 'Using Organic Fertilizers Responsibly', '**Organic fertilizers** can provide nutrients and organic matter, but organic does not mean unlimited or automatically risk-free. Using too much fertilizer, even if it comes from natural materials, can harm plants and the environment.\n\n## Responsible Use of Organic Fertilizers\n\nResponsible gardeners should:\n\n- **Use properly prepared materials**. Make sure compost, manure, and other organic fertilizers are properly decomposed before applying them to plants.\n- **Apply appropriate amounts**. Too much fertilizer can cause nutrient buildup, damage plant roots, or affect plant growth.\n- **Consider the needs of the crop**. Different plants have different nutrient requirements, so fertilizer application should be adjusted based on the type and growth stage of the plant.\n- **Avoid contaminated materials**. Do not use organic waste that may contain harmful chemicals, plastics, disease-causing organisms, or other contaminants.\n- **Prevent fertilizer from entering waterways**. Excess fertilizer can be carried by rainwater into drains, rivers, and other water sources, where it can contribute to water pollution.\n- **Observe how plants respond**. Watch for signs such as yellowing leaves, weak growth, leaf burn, or excessive growth, which may indicate nutrient problems.\n- **Reuse suitable organic waste whenever possible**. Kitchen scraps, plant residues, and other suitable biodegradable materials can be converted into compost or other organic fertilizers.\n- **Store fertilizers properly**. Keep organic fertilizers in a covered, dry, and safe area to prevent contamination and unwanted runoff.\n- **Use clean tools and containers**. Equipment used for preparing or applying fertilizers should be kept clean to prevent contamination.\n- **Follow proper preparation methods**. Liquid fertilizers, compost teas, and fermented organic fertilizers should be prepared using appropriate materials and methods before use.\n\n## Why Responsible Use Matters\n\nUsing organic fertilizers responsibly helps maintain healthy soil, healthy plants, and a cleaner environment. Organic fertilizers can improve soil structure and add nutrients, but they should be used carefully to avoid nutrient imbalance and pollution.\n\nBy practicing proper fertilizer management, urban gardeners can reduce organic waste, improve soil health, support plant growth, and make urban farming more sustainable.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 5, '2026-08-08 10:02:19'),
(62, 22, 'Understanding Fertilizer Application', '*Fertilizers* provide nutrients that plants need for growth and development. However, simply adding fertilizer to the soil does **not** guarantee healthy plants. Fertilizer must be applied properly to ensure that plants can benefit from the nutrients while reducing unnecessary waste and potential environmental problems.\n\n**Fertilizer** application refers to the process of placing or adding fertilizer to the soil or growing area so that nutrients can become available to plants.\n\nOrganic fertilizers such as **compost**, **vermicompost**, and properly treated **animal manure** can provide nutrients and organic matter. However, their nutrient content can vary depending on their source and how they were prepared.\n\n## Why Proper Application Matters\n\nProper fertilizer application helps gardeners:\n     - Provide nutrients according to plant needs\n     - Avoid unnecessary fertilizer use\n     - Reduce the risk of nutrient buildup\n     - Support healthy plant growth\n     - Reduce fertilizer waste\n     - Protect soil and nearby water sources\n\nApplying **too little** fertilizer may not provide enough nutrients when supplementation is needed. On the other hand, applying **too much** fertilizer can cause nutrient problems and may harm plants or contribute to environmental pollution.\n\n## Fertilizer Does Not Replace Other Plant Needs\n\nFertilizer is only one part of plant care.\n\nPlants also need:\n      - Adequate sunlight\n      - Appropriate water\n      - Suitable soil\n      - Proper drainage\n      - Appropriate temperature\n      - Sufficient growing space\n      - Protection from pests and diseases\n\nIf a plant is unhealthy because of poor watering or insufficient sunlight, adding more fertilizer may **not** solve the problem.\n\n## Before Applying Fertilizer\n\nBefore applying fertilizer, gardeners should ask:\n     - Does the plant actually need additional nutrients?\n     - What nutrients does the plant require?\n     - What is the condition of the soil?\n     - What type of fertilizer is available?\n     - How much fertilizer should be applied?\n     - Is the fertilizer properly prepared and suitable for the crop?\n\n**Remember**\n\nWhenever possible, soil testing can help determine soil nutrient conditions and guide fertilizer decisions.\nMore fertilizer does not always mean healthier plants.\nThe goal is to provide the appropriate amount of nutrients when they are needed.\n\n[checkpoint:eyJ0eXBlIjoidHJ1ZV9mYWxzZSIsInF1ZXN0aW9uIjoiRmVydGlsaXplciBhcHBsaWNhdGlvbiBpcyByZW1vdmluZyBudXRyaWVudHMgZnJvbSBwbGFudHMiLCJvcHRpb25zIjp7IkEiOiJUcnVlIiwiQiI6IkZhbHNlIiwiQyI6IiIsIkQiOiIifSwiY29ycmVjdCI6WyJCIl19]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 1, '2026-08-10 00:26:45'),
(63, 22, 'When and Where to Apply Fertilizer', 'The *timing* and *placement* of fertilizer can affect how effectively plants use nutrients.\nGardeners should consider the plant\'s growth stage, soil condition, type of fertilizer, and environmental conditions before applying fertilizer.\n\n## When Should Fertilizer Be Applied?\n\nThere is no single schedule that works for every plant and every fertilizer.\n\nThe appropriate timing depends on:\n     - Type of crop\n     - Growth stage\n     - Soil condition\n     - Type of fertilizer\n     - Nutrient requirements\n     - Weather and environmental conditions\n\nSome fertilizers release nutrients gradually, while others make nutrients available more quickly.\n\n## During Planting\n\nSome organic fertilizers or soil amendments can be incorporated into the soil before or during planting.\nFor example, mature compost can be mixed into the growing medium before seedlings are transplanted.\n\n## During Plant Growth\n\nAdditional fertilizer may sometimes be applied while plants are growing when their nutrient needs require it.\nGardeners should avoid applying fertilizer automatically. Instead, they should **observe** the plants and consider the soil and crop requirements.\n\n## Avoid Applying Fertilizer During Heavy Rain\n\nApplying fertilizer immediately before heavy rainfall can increase the risk of nutrients being washed away.\nRunoff can carry nutrients into drainage systems, streams, rivers, and other water sources.\n\n## Where Should Fertilizer Be Applied?\n\nFertilizer should generally be placed where plant roots can access the nutrients.\nFor established plants, fertilizer can be applied around the root zone rather than directly against the plant stem.\n\n## Avoid Fertilizer Contact With Stems and Leaves\n\nSome concentrated fertilizers may damage plant tissues when placed directly against stems or leaves.\nFollow the instructions appropriate for the fertilizer being used.\n\n## Watering After Application\n\nDepending on the fertilizer type and application method, watering may help move nutrients into the soil.\nHowever, excessive watering can also increase nutrient runoff.\nGardeners should **follow** the recommended instructions for the fertilizer being used.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 2, '2026-08-10 00:41:55'),
(64, 22, 'Methods of Applying Organic Fertilizer', 'There are several ways to apply organic fertilizers. The appropriate method depends on the type of fertilizer, the crop, and the growing conditions.\n\n## 1. Mixing With Soil\n\nCompost and other suitable organic amendments can be **mixed** into soil before planting.\nThis method distributes the material throughout the growing area and incorporates organic matter into the soil.\n\n**Example:**\nBefore planting lettuce, a gardener can incorporate mature compost into the garden bed according to the needs of the soil and crop.\n\n## 2. Top Dressing\n\nTop dressing involves placing a suitable organic fertilizer or soil amendment on the surface of the soil around an established plant.\nThe material should not be piled directly against the plant stem.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA gardener can place a layer of mature compost around the root zone of an established vegetable plant.\n\n## 3. Side Dressing\n\nSide dressing involves placing fertilizer alongside growing plants rather than directly on the plant.\nThis method can provide nutrients near the plant\'s root zone while keeping the fertilizer away from the stem.\n\n## 4. Liquid Application\n\nSome fertilizers can be prepared or supplied in liquid form.\nLiquid fertilizers should be applied according to reliable instructions because their concentration can vary.\nA solution that is too concentrated may damage plants.\n\n## Choosing the Appropriate Method\n\nBefore applying fertilizer, consider:\n     - The fertilizer type\n     - The crop\n     - Plant growth stage\n     - Soil condition\n     - Recommended application method\n     - Amount of fertilizer required\n\n**Important Reminder**\n\nNever assume that all organic fertilizers should be applied in the same way.\nCompost, vermicompost, manure, and liquid fertilizers have different characteristics and application requirements.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 3, '2026-08-10 00:52:28'),
(65, 22, 'Applying the Right Amount of Fertilizer', 'One of the most important parts of fertilizer application is determining the appropriate amount.\n\nUsing more fertilizer does not necessarily mean plants will grow better.\n\n## Why Is the Amount Important?\n\nPlants require nutrients in appropriate amounts.\n\nApplying too little fertilizer may not provide enough nutrients when supplementation is necessary. Applying too much can cause:\n- Nutrient buildup\n- Root damage\n- Poor plant growth\n- Nutrient imbalance\n- Fertilizer waste\n- Nutrient runoff\n- Environmental problems\n\n## Follow Recommended Instructions\n\nDifferent fertilizers have different nutrient concentrations.\n\nFor commercial fertilizers, gardeners should follow the product label and recommended application rates.\n\nFor compost and other organic materials, the appropriate amount depends on factors such as the crop, soil condition, material quality, and intended use.\n\n## Consider the Plant\n\nDifferent plants have different nutrient requirements.\n\nFor example, a leafy vegetable and a fruiting plant may have different nutrient needs.\n\nThe plant\'s growth stage can also affect its nutrient requirements.\n\n## Consider the Soil\n\nSoil that already contains sufficient nutrients may not require additional fertilizer.\n\nIf possible, soil testing can provide information about nutrient levels and help gardeners make better fertilizer decisions.\n\n## Avoid the \"More Is Better\" Mindset\n\nA common mistake among beginner gardeners is assuming:\n\nMore fertilizer = Faster growth = Healthier plants\n\nThis is not always true.\nA better approach is:\n\nPlant Needs + Soil Condition + Appropriate Fertilizer + Proper Amount = Responsible Fertilizer Use\n\n## Signs of Possible Fertilizer Problems\n\nPlants may show signs of nutrient imbalance or fertilizer injury.\n\nPossible signs include:\n     - Leaf burn\n     - Yellowing or unusual leaf color\n     - Weak or abnormal growth\n     - Wilting\n     - Poor root development\n\nThese symptoms can have many possible causes, so gardeners should not automatically assume that fertilizer is the problem.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 4, '2026-08-10 01:03:08'),
(66, 22, 'Responsible Fertilizer Application', 'Proper fertilizer application is not only about helping plants grow. It is also about protecting soil, water, people, and the surrounding environment.\n\n## Avoid Nutrient Runoff\n\nWhen excessive fertilizer is applied, rainwater or irrigation water can carry nutrients away from the garden.\n\nThese nutrients may enter:\n     - Drainage systems\n     - Streams\n     - Rivers\n     - Ponds\n     - Other water sources\n\nNutrient pollution can negatively affect aquatic ecosystems.\n\nResponsible fertilizer application helps reduce this risk.\n\n## Prevent Contamination\n\nOrganic fertilizers should be prepared, stored, and applied properly.\n\nGardeners should avoid using materials that may contain:\n     - Harmful chemicals\n     - Plastics\n     - Contaminated waste\n     - Disease-causing organisms\n     - Other unsafe materials\n\nThis is especially important when growing food crops.\n\n## Store Fertilizers Properly\n\nFertilizers should be stored in a suitable location that is:\n     - Dry\n     - Covered\n     - Secure\n     - Protected from excessive rain\n     - Away from waterways\n     - Away from children and pets\n\nProper storage reduces contamination and accidental runoff.\n\n## Observe the Plants\n\nAfter applying fertilizer, gardeners should monitor their plants.\n\nLook for:\n      - Healthy new growth\n      - Normal leaf color\n      - Strong stems\n      - Healthy roots\n      - Signs of fertilizer injury\n\nIf unusual symptoms appear, gardeners should investigate other possible causes instead of immediately adding more fertilizer.\n\n## Sustainable Fertilizer Use\n\nResponsible fertilizer application supports sustainable urban farming by:\n     - Reducing fertilizer waste\n     - Supporting healthy plant growth\n     - Maintaining soil quality\n     - Reducing nutrient runoff\n     - Recycling suitable organic materials\n     - Protecting nearby water sources\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 5, '2026-08-10 01:15:04'),
(67, 23, 'Understanding Soil', '**Soil** is an important part of plant growth. It provides plants with *support*, *water*, *air*, and *nutrients* needed for healthy development. In urban gardening, understanding soil is especially important because plants are often grown in containers, raised beds, rooftops, balconies, and other limited spaces.\n\n## What Is Soil?\n\nSoil is a mixture of mineral particles, organic matter, water, air, and living organisms.\nThe proportions of these components can vary depending on the type of soil.\n\n## Main Components of Soil\n\n1. **Mineral particles** come from weathered rocks and make up much of the physical structure of soil.\n\n2. **Organic matter** consists of decomposed plant and animal materials. It can help improve soil structure and support soil organisms.\n\n3. **Water** is stored in soil spaces and is important for plant growth.\n\n4. **Air** occupies some of the spaces between soil particles and provides oxygen needed by plant roots and soil organisms.\n\n5. **Living organisms** include microorganisms, earthworms, and other organisms that contribute to soil processes.\n\n## Why Is Healthy Soil Important?\n\nHealthy soil can:\n     - Support plant roots\n     - Store and provide water\n     - Supply nutrients\n     - Allow air to reach roots\n     - Support beneficial organisms\n     - Provide suitable conditions for plant growth\n\nPoor soil conditions can make it difficult for plants to grow properly.\n\n## Soil Texture\n\nSoil texture refers to the relative amounts of sand, silt, and clay particles in soil.\n\n1. **Sandy Soil** has relatively large particles and usually drains water quickly.\n2. **Clay Soil** contains very small particles and can hold considerable water, but it may drain slowly and become compacted.\n3. **Loamy Soil** contains a mixture of sand, silt, and clay and is often suitable for many garden plants when properly managed.\n\nRemember\n\nGood soil is not simply \"**dirt**.\" It is a living system that provides plants with support, water, air, and nutrients.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:eyJ0eXBlIjoibXVsdGlfc2VsZWN0IiwicXVlc3Rpb24iOiJXaGljaCBzb2lsIHR5cGUgZ2VuZXJhbGx5IGhhcyB2ZXJ5IHNtYWxsIHBhcnRpY2xlcyBhbmQgY2FuIGhvbGQgY29uc2lkZXJhYmxlIHdhdGVyPyIsIm9wdGlvbnMiOnsiQSI6IlNhbmR5IHNvaWwiLCJCIjoiQ2xheSBzb2lsIiwiQyI6IkdyYXZlbCIsIkQiOiJSb2NreSBzb2lsIn0sImNvcnJlY3QiOlsiQiJdfQ==]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 1, '2026-08-10 04:46:41'),
(68, 23, 'Assessing Soil Condition', 'Before planting, gardeners should examine the condition of the soil. Understanding the soil can help determine whether it needs improvement before crops are planted.\n\n## What Should Be Checked?\n\n**1. Soil Texture** - Feel the soil to determine whether it is mostly sandy, sticky and clay-like, or somewhere in between.\nTexture affects drainage, water storage, and root growth.\n\n**2. Soil Moisture** - Soil should contain enough moisture for plants but should not remain excessively wet.\nWaterlogged soil can reduce the amount of air available to plant roots.\n\n**3. Drainage** - Good drainage allows excess water to move away from the root zone.\nPoor drainage can cause water to accumulate around roots.\n\n**4. Soil Structure** - Soil structure refers to how individual soil particles group together.\nSoil with good structure can provide spaces for water and air while allowing roots to grow.\n\n**5. Organic Matter** - Organic matter can improve soil properties and provide a food source for soil organisms.\nCompost and other properly prepared organic materials can be used to increase organic matter.\n\n**6. Soil pH** - Soil pH indicates how acidic or alkaline the soil is.\nDifferent plants grow best within different pH ranges.\n\nA soil test can help determine soil pH and nutrient conditions more accurately.\n\n## Signs of Poor Soil Condition\n\nPossible signs include:\n - Very compacted soil\n - Poor drainage\n - Excessively dry soil\n - Water remaining on the surface\n - Weak or poorly developed plants\n - Very little organic matter\n\nThese signs do not always have a single cause, so gardeners should consider several factors before changing the soil.\n\n## Simple Soil Observation\n\nObserve whether it:\n\n - Falls apart easily\n - Forms a loose ball\n - Stays tightly clumped\n\nThis provides a basic observation of **soil texture** and **moisture**, although laboratory testing provides more accurate information.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 2, '2026-08-10 04:48:25'),
(69, 23, 'Preparing Soil Before Planting', 'Once the soil condition has been assessed, gardeners can prepare the growing area for planting. Proper preparation creates a more suitable environment for plant roots.\n\n## Remove Weeds and Debris\n\n**Step 1**: Remove Weeds and Debris\n\nRemove unwanted weeds, large stones, plastic, and other debris from the growing area.\nWeeds can compete with crops for water, nutrients, sunlight, and space.\n\n**Step 2**: Loosen Compacted Soil\n\nCompacted soil can restrict root growth and reduce spaces for air and water.\nGardeners can gently loosen the soil using appropriate hand tools.\n\nAvoid excessive digging or working soil when it is very wet because this can damage soil structure.\n\n**Step 3**: Add Suitable Organic Matter\n\nProperly prepared compost or other suitable organic materials can be incorporated into soil when appropriate.\nOrganic matter can help improve soil structure and contribute nutrients.\nHowever, not all organic materials are suitable for direct application.\n\n**Step 4**: Improve Drainage\n\nIf soil drains poorly, gardeners may need to improve the growing setup.\nFor container gardens, drainage holes should allow excess water to escape.\nRaised beds can also help manage drainage in some urban gardening situations.\n\n**Step 5**: Level the Growing Area\n\nAfter preparing the soil, the growing surface can be leveled so that planting is easier and water can be distributed more evenly.\n\n**Step 6**: Avoid Soil Compaction\n\nAvoid repeatedly walking on garden beds or pressing down heavily on the soil.\nCompaction can reduce pore spaces needed for air, water, and roots.\n\n## Preparing Soil for Containers\n\nContainer-grown plants need suitable growing media rather than simply filling a container with heavy garden soil.\n\nA suitable growing medium should generally provide:\n- Root support\n- Adequate drainage\n- Moisture retention\n- Air spaces\n- Nutrients or access to nutrients\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 3, '2026-08-10 04:57:22'),
(70, 23, 'Choosing and Preparing Growing Media', 'Urban gardeners may grow plants in containers, pots, raised beds, recycled containers, and other limited spaces.\nThe material used to grow plants is called the **growing medium**.\n\n## Garden Soil\n\nGarden soil can contain minerals, organic matter, water, air, and living organisms.\nHowever, heavy garden soil may not always be suitable for containers because it can become compacted and drain poorly.\n\n## Compost\n\nMature compost can provide organic matter and contribute nutrients.\nIt can be incorporated into growing media in appropriate amounts.\n\n## Coco Coir\n\nCoco coir is a material made from coconut fiber.\nIt can help retain moisture while providing a relatively light growing medium.\nCoco coir does not provide all nutrients needed by plants, so it may need to be combined with other materials and appropriate fertilizers.\n\n## Perlite\n\nPerlite is a lightweight material commonly used in growing media.\nIt can help improve drainage and maintain air spaces around plant roots.\n\n## Vermiculite\n\nVermiculite is another lightweight material used in some growing media.\nIt can help retain moisture and improve the physical properties of a growing mixture.\n\n## Combining Materials\n\nGardeners may combine different materials to create a growing medium suitable for their crops.\n\nFor example:\n\nOrganic Matter + Moisture-Retaining Material + Aeration/Drainage Material\n\nThe exact mixture should depend on the crop and growing conditions.\n\n## Container Preparation\n\nBefore planting:\n- Choose an appropriately sized container.\n- Make sure the container has drainage holes.\n- Clean the container when necessary.\n- Prepare a suitable growing medium.\n- Fill the container without excessively compacting the medium.\n- Leave enough space for watering.\n- Plant the crop at the appropriate depth.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 4, '2026-08-10 05:06:35'),
(71, 23, 'Maintaining Healthy Soil', 'Preparing soil is only the beginning. **Soil** should continue to be managed throughout the growing season.\n\n## Add Organic Matter\n\nAdding appropriate amounts of mature compost or other suitable organic matter can help maintain soil quality.\nOrganic matter can contribute to soil structure and support soil organisms.\n\n## Use Mulch\n\nMulch is a material placed on the soil surface.\n\nExamples include:\n- Dried leaves\n- Straw\n- Wood-based materials suitable for mulch\n- Other appropriate organic materials\n\nMulch can help:\n- Reduce moisture loss\n- Reduce soil erosion\n- Suppress some weeds\n- Protect the soil surface\n\n## Avoid Soil Compaction\n\nAvoid unnecessary walking or heavy pressure on garden beds.\nCompacted soil can reduce pore spaces and make it more difficult for roots to access air and water.\n\n## Practice Crop Rotation\n\nGrowing different crops in sequence can help manage some soil and pest problems.\nFor example, gardeners can avoid planting the same crop family repeatedly in exactly the same location when space allows.\n\n## Prevent Erosion\n\nUrban gardens can experience soil loss due to heavy rainfall or water runoff.\nVegetation, mulch, proper drainage, and appropriate garden design can help protect the soil surface.\n\n## Avoid Excessive Chemical and Fertilizer Use\n\nUsing inputs responsibly helps reduce the risk of nutrient buildup and environmental contamination.\nOrganic fertilizers should also be used appropriately. \"Organic\" does not mean that unlimited amounts are safe.\n\n## Recycle Organic Materials\n\nSuitable plant materials and other biodegradable organic materials can be composted and returned to the garden.\n\nThis creates a useful cycle:\n\nOrganic Waste → Compost → Healthy Soil → Plants → Organic Waste\n\n## Monitor Soil and Plants\n\nYou should regularly observe:\n- Soil moisture\n- Drainage\n- Plant growth\n- Leaf color\n- Root development\n- Signs of pests or disease\n- Soil surface condition\n\nRegular observation allows gardeners to respond to problems before they become severe.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 5, '2026-08-10 05:18:06');
INSERT INTO `lessons` (`lesson_id`, `module_id`, `title`, `content`, `lesson_order`, `created_at`) VALUES
(72, 24, 'Understanding Seeds and Seed Selection', '**Seeds** are one of the most common ways to start plants. A seed contains the structures needed to develop into a new plant when suitable conditions are provided.\nSuccessful gardening begins with selecting seeds that are appropriate for the growing environment.\nIn urban gardening, space, sunlight, temperature, container size, and local growing conditions can affect which crops are suitable.\n\n## What Is a Seed?\n\nA seed is a reproductive structure produced by many plants. It contains a developing plant embryo and stored resources that support early growth.\nWhen a viable seed receives suitable conditions such as moisture, oxygen, and an appropriate temperature, it can begin to **germinate**.\n\n## Why Is Seed Selection Important?\n\nChoosing suitable seeds can improve the chances of successful germination and healthy plant growth.\n\nWhen selecting seeds, consider:\n- Crop type\n- Growing space\n- Climate and temperature\n- Available sunlight\n- Expected harvest time\n- Seed quality\n- Seed age and storage condition\n- Suitability for containers or garden beds\n\n## Choose Crops Suitable for Your Space\n\nUrban gardens often have limited space.\n\nFor example, leafy vegetables such as lettuce and spinach can be suitable for containers because they can be grown in relatively small spaces.\n\nLarger crops may require more space, support structures, or larger containers.\n\nBefore purchasing seeds, consider how much space the mature plant will require.\n\n## Check Seed Information\n\nSeed packets may provide useful information such as:\n\n- Crop variety\n- Days to germination\n- Days to maturity\n- Recommended planting depth\n- Spacing requirements\n- Sunlight requirements\n- Planting season\n- Expected plant size\n\nReading the seed packet helps gardeners understand how to grow the selected crop.\n\n## Seed Quality\n\nGood-quality seeds should generally be:\n- Suitable for the intended crop\n- Properly stored\n- Free from visible damage or contamination\n- Viable and capable of germination\n\nSeeds that have been exposed to excessive heat, moisture, or poor storage conditions may have reduced viability.\n\n## Seed Viability\n\nSeed viability refers to whether a seed is capable of developing into a living plant under suitable conditions.\n\n**Not** every seed will successfully **germinate**.\n\nGermination rates can vary depending on the crop, seed age, storage conditions, and environmental conditions.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 1, '2026-08-10 05:41:23'),
(73, 24, 'Preparing for Seed Starting', 'Before sowing seeds, gardeners should prepare suitable containers, growing media, water, and a location where seedlings can receive appropriate conditions.\nGood preparation can make seed starting easier and reduce problems during germination.\n\n## Choosing a Container\n\nSeeds can be started in:\n- Seed trays\n- Small pots\n- Reused containers\n- Nursery containers\n- Other suitable planting containers\n\nContainers should be clean and should provide adequate drainage.\n\n## Why Drainage Matters\n\nExcess water can reduce the amount of oxygen available around seeds and young roots.\n\nPoor drainage may increase the risk of seed or root problems.\n\nContainers should have drainage holes that allow excess water to escape.\n\n## Preparing the Growing Medium\n\nSeeds need a suitable medium for germination and early root development.\n\nA good seed-starting medium should generally be:\n- Loose\n- Well-drained\n- Moisture-retentive\n- Free from large debris\n- Suitable for young roots\n\nGarden soil that is heavy or compacted may not be ideal for starting seeds in containers.\nCommercial seed-starting mixes or suitable prepared growing media may be used.\n\n## Preparing the Growing Area\n\nSeedlings need suitable environmental conditions.\n\nConsider:\n- Light\n- Temperature\n- Moisture\n- Air circulation\n- Protection from heavy rain\n- Protection from strong winds\n\nA bright location with suitable conditions can help young seedlings develop properly.\n\n## Labeling Seeds\n\nWhen starting several crops, label each container.\n\nA label can include:\n- Plant name\n- Variety\n- Date planted\n\nLabeling prevents confusion and helps gardeners monitor growth.\n\n## Preparing Seeds\n\nMost seeds can be planted directly according to the instructions on the seed packet.\n\nSome seeds may benefit from special preparation, such as soaking or other treatments, depending on the crop.\n\nGardeners should follow reliable instructions for the specific seed rather than applying the same treatment to every type of seed.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 2, '2026-08-10 06:52:11'),
(74, 24, 'Sowing Seeds Properly', 'After preparing the containers and growing medium, the next step is to sow the seeds.\n\nProper sowing helps provide seeds with the conditions they need to germinate.\n\n## Follow the Recommended Planting Depth\n\nDifferent seeds should be planted at different depths.\n\nSmall seeds are generally planted more shallowly than larger seeds.\n\nThe recommended depth can usually be found on the seed packet.\n\nPlanting seeds too deeply may make it difficult for young seedlings to emerge.\n\n## Spacing\n\nSeeds should be spaced appropriately to reduce competition as seedlings develop.\n\nCrowded seedlings may compete for:\n- Light\n- Water\n- Nutrients\n- Growing space\n\nThe recommended spacing depends on the crop.\n\n## Sowing Steps\n\nA basic seed-sowing process is:\n\n1. Prepare the container.\n2. Fill it with suitable growing medium.\n3. Moisten the growing medium.\n4. Make a small hole or planting space at the recommended depth.\n5. Place the seed in the hole.\n6. Cover the seed lightly with growing medium.\n7. Water gently.\n8. Label the container.\n9. Place it in a suitable growing location.\n\n## Watering Newly Sown Seeds\n\nSeeds need moisture to begin germination.\n\nHowever, excessive water can cause problems.\n\nWater gently so that seeds are not washed out of the growing medium.\n\nThe growing medium should generally remain appropriately moist rather than continuously saturated.\n\n## Germination\n\n**Germination** begins when a viable seed starts developing into a seedling under suitable conditions.\n\nThe seed absorbs water, and the embryo begins to grow.\n\nThe first root, called the **radicle**, emerges first in many plants. The developing shoot then grows upward toward the surface.\n\nGermination time varies between crops.\n\nSome seeds germinate within a few days, while others may take considerably longer.\n\n## Do Not Disturb Germinating Seeds\n\nOnce seeds have been planted, avoid repeatedly digging them up to check whether they have germinated.\n\nInstead, observe the surface and maintain appropriate moisture and environmental conditions.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 3, '2026-08-10 06:57:45'),
(75, 24, 'The process of adding fertilizer to soil', 'Once seeds germinate, young seedlings require proper care to continue growing.\n\nThe early stage of plant development is important because seedlings are generally more sensitive to environmental conditions than established plants.\n\n## Provide Appropriate Light\n\nAfter germination, seedlings need sufficient **light** for healthy development.\n\nInsufficient light can cause seedlings to become:\n - Tall and thin\n - Weak\n - Pale\n\nPlace seedlings where they can receive appropriate light based on the crop\'s requirements.\n\n## Provide Appropriate Water\n\nSeedlings have small root systems, so they can dry out more quickly than established plants.\n\nCheck the growing medium regularly.\n\nWater gently when needed.\n\nAvoid keeping the growing medium continuously saturated.\n\n## Provide Suitable Temperature\n\nDifferent crops have different temperature requirements for germination and early growth.\n\nSeedlings should be protected from extreme heat or cold when necessary.\n\n## Provide Air Circulation\n\nGood air circulation can help reduce excessive humidity around seedlings.\n\nAvoid overcrowding seedlings because dense growth can create conditions that encourage some diseases.\n\n## Thinning Seedlings\n\nIf several seedlings emerge too close together, they may need to be thinned.\n\n**Thinning** means removing some seedlings so that the remaining plants have enough space to grow.\n\nWhen thinning, keep the healthiest seedlings when appropriate.\n\n## Hardening Off\n\nSeedlings started indoors or in protected environments may need to be gradually exposed to outdoor conditions before transplanting.\n\nThis process is called hardening off.\n\nIt helps seedlings adjust to changes in sunlight, temperature, wind, and other outdoor conditions.\n\nBasic Hardening-Off Process:\n\n1. Begin with short periods outdoors.\n2. Place seedlings in a sheltered location.\n3. Gradually increase their exposure to outdoor conditions.\n4. Protect them from extreme weather.\n5. Transplant once they have adjusted sufficiently.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 4, '2026-08-10 07:13:01'),
(76, 24, 'Common Seed Starting Problems and Transplant Preparation', 'Even when gardeners follow proper procedures, seeds and seedlings can experience problems.\n\nLearning how to identify common problems can help gardeners respond appropriately.\n\n## Problem 1: Seeds Do Not Germinate\n\nPossible causes include:\n- Poor seed viability\n- Incorrect planting depth\n- Incorrect temperature\n- Insufficient moisture\n- Excessive moisture\n- Poor seed quality\n- Inappropriate growing conditions\n\nBefore replanting, check whether the conditions are suitable for the specific crop.\n\n## Problem 2: Seedlings Become Long and Weak\n\nThis is often associated with insufficient light.\n\nThe seedlings may stretch toward a light source and develop weak stems.\n\nMove them to a location with more appropriate light if possible.\n\n## Problem 3: Seedlings Collapse at the Soil Surface\n\nYoung seedlings that suddenly become weak or collapse may be experiencing a disease or environmental problem.\n\nExcess moisture, poor air circulation, contaminated growing media, and crowded seedlings can contribute to some seedling diseases.\n\nMaintain appropriate moisture, cleanliness, and air circulation.\n\n## Problem 4: Yellowing Seedlings\n\nYellow leaves can have several possible causes, including:\n\n- Watering problems\n- Nutrient issues\n- Poor root conditions\n- Insufficient light\n- Disease\n\nDo not immediately assume that fertilizer is needed.\n\nFirst check the growing conditions.\n\n## Problem 5: Overwatering\n\nToo much water can reduce oxygen around roots and increase the risk of root problems.\n\nAllow excess water to drain from containers.\n\n## Preparing Seedlings for Transplanting\n\nWhen seedlings become sufficiently established, they may be transplanted into larger containers, garden beds, or other growing areas.\n\nBefore transplanting, make sure:\n- The seedling is sufficiently developed.\n- The new growing area is prepared.\n- The soil or growing medium is suitable.\n- The container is appropriately sized.\n- The plant has been hardened off when necessary.\n\n## Transplanting Steps\n\n1. Prepare the new planting area.\n2 Water the seedling if appropriate.\n3. Carefully remove the seedling while minimizing root disturbance.\n4. Place it at an appropriate depth.\n5. Fill the space around the roots with growing medium.\n6. Water gently.\n7. Protect the seedling while it adjusts.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:eyJ0eXBlIjoibXVsdGlfc2VsZWN0IiwicXVlc3Rpb24iOiJXaGF0IGNhbiBjYXVzZSBzZWVkbGluZ3MgdG8gYmVjb21lIHRhbGwgYW5kIHdlYWs/Iiwib3B0aW9ucyI6eyJBIjoiUHJvcGVyIHNwYWNpbmciLCJCIjoiSW5zdWZmaWNpZW50IGxpZ2h0IiwiQyI6IlN1aXRhYmxlIGxpZ2h0IiwiRCI6IkFwcHJvcHJpYXRlIHdhdGVyaW5nIn0sImNvcnJlY3QiOlsiQiJdfQ==]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 5, '2026-08-10 07:21:54'),
(77, 25, 'Understanding Harvesting and Crop Maturity', 'Harvesting is the process of removing mature crops from the plant or growing area for consumption, storage, processing, or other uses.\n\nKnowing **when to harvest** is important because harvesting too early or too late can affect the quality, taste, appearance, and storage life of produce.\n\nDifferent crops have different signs of maturity. Gardeners should learn the characteristics of the specific crop they are growing.\n\n## What Is Crop Maturity?\n\nCrop maturity refers to the stage when a crop has developed sufficiently for its intended purpose.\n\nThe appropriate harvest stage depends on the crop.\n\nFor example:\n- Leafy vegetables may be harvested when their leaves have reached a suitable size.\n- Root crops may be harvested when their roots have reached an appropriate size.\n- Fruiting crops may be harvested based on size, color, firmness, or other maturity indicators.\n\n## Signs That Crops May Be Ready\n\nYou should look for:\n- Appropriate size\n- Expected color\n- Firmness\n- Leaf development\n- Root size\n- Flower or fruit development\n- Information provided for the specific crop variety\n\n## Harvesting Too Early\n\nHarvesting too early may result in:\n- Smaller produce\n- Less-developed flavor\n- Lower yield\n- Reduced quality\n\nHowever, some vegetables are intentionally harvested while young and tender.\n\n## Harvesting Too Late\n\nLeaving crops too long can also affect quality.\n\nPossible problems include:\n- Tough leaves\n- Overripe fruits\n- Reduced tenderness\n- Increased pest damage\n- Spoilage\n\nThe best harvest stage therefore depends on the crop and how the produce will be used.\n\n## Harvest Time\n\nFor many crops, harvesting during cooler parts of the day can help reduce heat stress and maintain freshness.\n\nGardeners should avoid harvesting produce when it is unnecessarily wet if this could increase handling or storage problems.\n\n## Harvest Planning\n\nBefore harvesting, prepare:\n- Clean harvesting tools\n- Clean containers\n- A suitable harvesting area\n- Storage or cooling space when needed\n\nBeing prepared helps prevent unnecessary handling and damage.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 1, '2026-08-10 07:54:55'),
(78, 25, 'Proper Harvesting Techniques', 'Different crops require different harvesting methods.\n\nUsing the **correct technique** can help reduce damage to the plant and harvested produce.\n\n## Use Clean Tools\n\nHarvesting tools such as scissors, knives, or pruning shears should be clean and suitable for the crop.\n\nDirty tools can transfer contaminants or plant pathogens.\n\n## Handle Produce Carefully\n\nAvoid throwing, dropping, squeezing, or stepping on harvested produce.\n\nBruises and cuts can make produce more vulnerable to spoilage.\n\n## Harvesting Leafy Vegetables\n\nLeafy vegetables such as lettuce and spinach can be harvested by removing mature leaves or harvesting the entire plant, depending on the crop and intended use.\n\nUse clean tools when cutting leaves or stems.\n\nAvoid unnecessary damage to remaining plant tissues when continued growth is desired.\n\n## Harvesting Root Crops\n\nRoot crops such as carrots and radishes are generally harvested by carefully loosening the surrounding soil and pulling the root from the ground.\n\nAvoid excessive force that could break or damage the edible portion.\n\n## Harvesting Fruiting Crops\n\nFruits such as tomatoes, peppers, and similar crops should be harvested according to their appropriate maturity stage.\n\nSome crops can be harvested by hand, while others may require clean cutting tools.\n\n## Do Not Overfill Containers\n\nHarvested produce should not be packed so tightly that lower layers are crushed.\n\nUse containers that provide enough space and support.\n\n## Avoid Unnecessary Handling\n\nThe more produce is handled, the greater the opportunity for bruising, contamination, and damage.\n\nHandle crops gently and only when necessary.\n\n## Basic Harvesting Process\n\nCheck Maturity → Prepare Clean Tools → Harvest Carefully → Place in Clean Container → Protect From Damage → Move to Proper Handling Area\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 2, '2026-08-10 07:59:00'),
(79, 25, 'Cleaning, Sorting, and Grading Produce', 'After harvesting, produce should be handled appropriately before storage or consumption.\n\nCleaning and sorting can help remove unwanted materials and separate damaged produce.\n\n## Initial Inspection\n\nAfter harvesting, inspect the produce for:\n- Soil\n- Damaged portions\n- Insects\n- Diseased material\n- Bruising\n- Foreign objects\n\nDamaged or diseased produce should be separated from healthy produce.\n\n## Cleaning Produce\n\nSome fresh produce may need to be cleaned before consumption.\n\nThe appropriate cleaning method depends on the crop and intended use.\n\nFor produce that will be eaten, follow safe food-handling practices and use clean water suitable for washing food.\n\nAvoid using soaps, detergents, or household cleaning chemicals on produce unless specifically intended and approved for food use.\n\n## Sorting\n\nSorting means separating produce according to characteristics such as:\n- Size\n- Quality\n- Maturity\n- Damage\n- Appearance\n\nFor example, undamaged tomatoes can be separated from bruised or damaged tomatoes.\n\n## Grading\n\nGrading is the process of grouping produce based on quality or specific standards.\n\nFor a small urban garden, gardeners may simply separate:\n- High-quality produce\n- Slightly damaged produce\n- Produce that should be used immediately\n- Produce that should be discarded because it is spoiled or unsafe\n\n## Why Sorting Matters\n\nSorting helps:\n- Protect healthy produce\n- Identify damaged crops\n- Reduce the spread of spoilage\n- Make storage more organized\n- Prioritize produce that needs to be consumed quickly\n\n## Do Not Wash Everything Immediately\n\nSome crops may have different storage requirements, and washing can sometimes increase moisture around produce and encourage spoilage.\n\nGardeners should follow crop-specific recommendations for washing and storage.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 3, '2026-08-10 08:02:04'),
(80, 25, 'Proper Storage of Harvested Produce', 'Proper storage helps maintain the quality and freshness of harvested crops.\n\nDifferent crops have different storage requirements.\n\n## Factors That Affect Storage\n\nImportant factors include:\n- Temperature\n- Humidity\n- Air circulation\n- Light\n- Moisture\n- Produce maturity\n- Storage container\n\n## Keep Produce in Appropriate Conditions\n\nSome crops remain fresh longer under cool conditions, while others can be damaged by excessively cold temperatures.\n\nGardeners should learn the appropriate storage requirements for the specific crop.\n\n## Avoid Excess Moisture\n\nExcess moisture can encourage the growth of microorganisms and increase spoilage.\n\nProduce should be stored in conditions appropriate for the crop, with suitable ventilation when needed.\n\n## Avoid Direct Sunlight\n\nDirect sunlight can increase temperature and accelerate deterioration for some harvested crops.\n\nKeep harvested produce in an appropriate shaded or storage area when necessary.\n\n## Separate Damaged Produce\n\nDamaged or spoiled produce should be separated from healthy produce.\n\nThis can help reduce the spread of spoilage.\n\n## Use Appropriate Containers\n\nStorage containers should be:\n- Clean\n- Suitable for the produce\n- Strong enough to protect the crops\n- Properly ventilated when required\n\nAvoid containers that can easily crush or damage delicate produce.\n\n## First In, First Out\n\nA simple storage practice is First In, First Out (FIFO).\n\nThis means using older harvested produce before newer produce when appropriate.\n\nFIFO can help reduce food waste.\n\n## Storage Examples\n\n**Leafy Vegetables**\n\nLeafy vegetables generally benefit from cool, moist conditions that help prevent excessive water loss.\n\n**Root Crops**\n\nSome root crops can be stored under cool conditions depending on the crop.\n\nRemove excess soil carefully when appropriate and avoid damaging the roots.\n\n**Tomatoes**\n\nTomatoes have different storage requirements depending on their maturity stage. Fully ripe tomatoes may need to be used relatively quickly to maintain quality.\n\n**Remember**\n\nThere is no single storage method that is appropriate for every crop.\n\nAlways consider the specific requirements of the harvested produce.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 4, '2026-08-10 08:07:30'),
(81, 25, 'Reducing Post-Harvest Losses and Food Waste', 'Post-harvest losses occur when harvested crops lose quality or become unusable after harvesting.\n\nLosses can happen during:\n- Harvesting\n- Handling\n- Cleaning\n- Sorting\n- Transportation\n- Storage\n- Preparation\n\nReducing these losses is an important part of sustainable urban farming.\n\n## Common Causes of Post-Harvest Loss\n\n**Physical Damage**\n\nDropping, squeezing, cutting, or crushing produce can cause bruising and damage.\n\n**Improper Storage**\n\nIncorrect temperature, humidity, or ventilation can cause produce to deteriorate more quickly.\n\n**Poor Hygiene**\n\nDirty tools, containers, hands, or surfaces can introduce contaminants.\n\n**Delayed Handling**\n\nLeaving harvested produce exposed to unsuitable conditions can reduce quality.\n\n**Overharvesting**\n\nHarvesting more produce than can be consumed, stored, or shared can lead to unnecessary waste.\n\n## How Can You Reduce Food Waste?\n\nYou can:\n- Harvest only what is ready.\n- Handle produce carefully.\n- Use clean tools and containers.\n- Sort produce after harvesting.\n- Store crops appropriately.\n- Use older produce first.\n- Share excess harvest.\n- Preserve suitable produce.\n- Compost suitable unavoidable plant waste.\n\n## Using Imperfect Produce\n\nNot all imperfect produce needs to be thrown away.\n\nProduce with minor cosmetic imperfections may still be suitable for consumption if it is safe and properly handled.\n\nFor example, a slightly irregular carrot may still be perfectly usable.\n\nHowever, produce that is spoiled, moldy, or otherwise unsafe should not be consumed.\n\n## Sharing Excess Harvest\n\nUrban gardeners may sometimes produce more crops than their household can consume.\n\nInstead of allowing suitable produce to spoil, gardeners can consider:\n- Sharing with family\n- Sharing with neighbors\n- Donating through appropriate community programs\n- Using produce in different meals\n- Preserving suitable crops\n\n## Composting Unusable Plant Material\n\nSome unavoidable plant waste can be composted when suitable.\n\nThis creates a cycle:\n\nGrow → Harvest → Eat → Compost Suitable Waste → Improve Soil → Grow Again\n\n## Sustainable Harvesting\n\nGood harvesting practices help ensure that the effort invested in growing crops is not wasted.\n\nBy harvesting at the right time, handling produce carefully, and storing it appropriately, urban gardeners can make better use of their harvest and reduce food waste.\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]\n\n[checkpoint:]', 5, '2026-08-10 08:11:26');

